{#
	The lead article on the posts index. Figma `featured-post` / `featured-card`
	on `blog / index` (21:107).

	Derived, not authored. Like `next-project`, `breadcrumbs` and `pagination`
	this comes from the query: the featured post is simply the newest one, and
	`index.twig` drops it from the list below so it is never shown twice. An
	authored "featured post" field would drift the moment something newer is
	published, and the drift would be silent — the index would keep promoting
	last spring's article with nobody noticing.

	`מומלץ` is a fixed label on derived navigation, in the same class as
	`הפרויקט הבא` in `next-project` — it names the slot, it is not content an
	editor chose.

	DOM order is text → media, the opposite of `post-item`. In Figma the canvas
	is LTR, so the image sits first (x=32, the left) and the body second
	(x=620, the right); under dir="rtl" the *first* child lands on the right,
	so the body has to come first to end up where the frame draws it. It also
	puts the title ahead of a decorative-by-position image in the reading order.
	Both files are correct — don't "fix" one to match the other.

	Params:
	  post  Timber\Post — the article to promote.
#}

{% if post %}
	<article class="featured-post">

		<div class="featured-post__body">
			<p class="featured-post__meta">
				מומלץ · <time datetime="{{ post.date('c') }}">{{ post.date('d.m.Y') }}</time>
			</p>

			{# h2: the page's h1 is the archive heading in the hero above. #}
			<h2 class="featured-post__title">
				<a class="featured-post__link" href="{{ post.link }}"><bdi>{{ post.title }}</bdi></a>
			</h2>

			{% if post.excerpt %}
				<p class="featured-post__excerpt"><bdi>{{ post.excerpt }}</bdi></p>
			{% endif %}
		</div>

		{# No thumbnail, no frame. Half a card of empty gradient is worse than a
		   body that uses the full width. #}
		{% if post.thumbnail %}
			{# No width/height. `.width` and `.height` are the *full* attachment's,
			   not the `large` file's, so the pair would describe a different
			   picture than the one in `src` — and the box is already fixed by the
			   container's aspect-ratio, so there is nothing for them to reserve. #}
			<div class="featured-post__media">
				<img
					src="{{ post.thumbnail.src('large') }}"
					alt="{{ post.thumbnail.alt|default(post.title) }}"
					loading="lazy"
					decoding="async"
				>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

	</article>
{% endif %}
